l index on `email` can't be used, so the database evaluates `lower()` on every row. This is the main cost.\n2. **Full scan of `orders`, or a hash join over it.** If `orders.user_id` has no index, each matching user forces a scan of `orders`. Without an index, the planner may build a hash join that reads all of `orders`. Primary keys are indexed automatically, but foreign keys usually aren't. I can't see the schema, so check this.\n3. **Wide row reads from `SELECT *`.** It pulls every column from both tables. That rules out index-only scans and widens the rows. It also returns duplicate column names (`id` appears in both tables), which can break clients that map columns by name.\n\n## Fixes\n\n**1. Index the lookup expression (PostgreSQL):**\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n```\nThe index has to use the same expression as the query.\n\nA rewrite would also fix this. Either store emails already lowercased and compare `u.email = 'a@example.org'`, or use `citext`. Both let a plain unique index on `email` serve the lookup. This also enforces case-insensitive uniqueness. In MySQL, the default collations are already case-insensitive, so drop `lower()` and use a plain index on `email`.\n\n**2. Index the join key:**\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n```\nWith this, the join becomes one index lookup on `users`, then one index range scan on `orders`.\n\n**3. Select only the columns you need:**\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM orders o\nJOIN users u ON u.id = o.user_id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\nThe column list above is a placeholder. Use your real columns. If the list is small and stable, you can add them with `INCLUDE` on the `orders` index to allow index-only scans.\n\n## Caveats\n- I haven't seen the schema, row counts, or `EXPLAIN` output. Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after to confirm the plan changes. On 